<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-determinant-wronskian" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Determinant and the Wronskian</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO + REASSURANCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We found two solutions to a system on the last page, and hinted that they can be combined
    to build the general solution. To make that precise, we need two more tools from linear
    algebra: the determinant and linear independence.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keep in mind that in a linear algebra course, these ideas get weeks of
    development and carry a lot of theory. We only need a working understanding for ODEs, so we
    won't go deep into the theory. If you haven't taken a linear algebra or matrices course,
    then you may find this section a little dense.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: DETERMINANT (2x2)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-determinant">
    <title>Determinant</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix in the form
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>, then the
        <term>determinant</term> of <m>A</m> is:
        <me>\det(A) = \begin{vmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    The straight bars mean we're taking the determinant of the matrix. There are a lot of 
    important interpretations and consequences of the determinant. What we need is the
    connection to linear independence.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: LINEAR INDEPENDENCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-linear-independence">
    <title>Linear Independence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Two vectors <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> are
        <term>linearly independent</term> if the <em>only</em> solution to:
        <me>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{0}</me>
        is when <m>c_1 = c_2 = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In general there is a lot to unpack with linear independence, but with just two vectors,
    <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> are linearly independent exactly when they are
    <alert>not multiples of each other</alert>. And when that happens, the linear combinations
    <m>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y}</m> realize every vector in the plane as you vary over
    all <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>. That is, every vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> can be built
    out of two linearly independent vectors.
  </p>

  <p>
    The determinant gives us a quick test for linear independence:
  </p>

  <fact xml:id="fact-det-independence">
    <title>Determinant Test for Independence</title>
    <p>
      The columns of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix are linearly independent if and only if
      <m>\det(A) \neq 0</m>.
    </p>
  </fact>
<p>We will apply this idea in the following example.</p>
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: COMPUTE A DETERMINANT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-determinant-independence">
    <title>Computing a Determinant</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the determinant of
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        Are the columns linearly independent?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Using the formula:
        <me>\det = \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 4 \end{vmatrix}
          = 1 \cdot 4 - 2 \cdot 3 = 4 - 6 = -2.</me>
        Since <m>-2 \neq 0</m>, the columns
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: DETERMINANT CHECKPOINT (MC)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Using the same process as the example, complete the following checkpoint.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-determinant-independence">
    <title>Determinant Checkpoint</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the determinant of
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 4 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        Are the columns linearly independent?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\det = 0</m>, so the columns are <em>not</em> linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! <m>\det = 1 \cdot 8 - 2 \cdot 4 = 8 - 8 = 0</m>, so the columns are not
            linearly independent. In fact, you can see it directly: the second column
            <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 8 \end{bmatrix}</m> is exactly <m>2</m> times the first
            column <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>. They're multiples of each other!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\det = 0</m>, so the columns <em>are</em> linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The determinant is right, but the conclusion is backwards! The test says the
            columns are linearly independent when <m>\det \neq 0</m>. Here <m>\det = 0</m>,
            so the columns are <em>not</em> independent. Indeed, the second column is <m>2</m>
            times the first.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\det = 16</m>, so the columns are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Check the formula: it's <m>ad - bc</m>, with a <em>minus</em> sign. Here that's
            <m>1 \cdot 8 - 2 \cdot 4 = 0</m>, not <m>8 + 8 = 16</m>. Since the determinant is
            <m>0</m>, the columns are not linearly independent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SUPERPOSITION AND THE BIG QUESTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Let's return to solutions of systems. As before, we are only going to scratch the surface
    of the underlying theory. Instead, most of our effort will go towards applying these ideas
    in future sections!
  </p>

  <fact xml:id="fact-superposition-systems">
    <title>The Principle of Superposition</title>
    <p>
      With <em>homogeneous</em> linear systems, we still have a <alert>principle of
      superposition</alert>. That is, if <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> are solutions to:
      <md>
        <mrow>x_1' \amp= p_{11}(t)x_1 + p_{12}(t)x_2</mrow>
        <mrow>x_2' \amp= p_{21}(t)x_1 + p_{22}(t)x_2</mrow>
      </md>
      (written in vector form as <m>\mathbf{x}' = P(t)\mathbf{x}</m>), then any linear
      combination:
      <me>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y}</me>
      is also a solution.
    </p>
  </fact>

  <p>
    (You can verify this by plugging <m>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y}</m> into the system,
    just like we did for second-order equations.)
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's connect this back to <xref ref="ex-verify-solutions-system"/>, where we verified
    that
    <me>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      \mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</me>
    are both solutions to the homogeneous system
    <m>\mathbf{x}' = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp -7 \end{bmatrix}\mathbf{x}</m>.
    The principle of superposition lets us build many more solutions out of these two.
  </p>

 <activity xml:id="act-superposition-family">
    <title>Superposition Checkpoint</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which of the following is an <em>infinite family</em> of solutions built from
        <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
              + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! This is <m>c_1\mathbf{y} + c_2\mathbf{z}</m>. By the principle of
            superposition, every choice of the constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> produces
            a solution, so this is infinitely many solutions all at once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}
              + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Look closely: these two vectors are not <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m>!
            Their components have been mixed together, and these mixed vectors are not
            solutions to the system, so superposition doesn't apply. Each solution keeps its
            own exponential in <em>both</em> components: <m>\mathbf{y}</m> is all
            <m>e^{2t}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> is all <m>e^{-5t}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 2e^{2t} \\ 3e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
              + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{-5t} \\ e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Careful: the components of each vector have been flipped upside down! The
            solution <m>\mathbf{y}</m> has <m>3e^{2t}</m> on top and <m>2e^{2t}</m> on the
            bottom (and similarly for <m>\mathbf{z}</m>). A flipped vector is generally
            <em>not</em> a solution anymore, so superposition only applies to the original
            <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </activity>
     
   

  <p>
    So from just two solutions, we instantly get infinitely many. This raises the question:
    when does <m>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y}</m> give us <em>all</em> of the
    solutions?
  </p>

  <p>
    It turns out this is when <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> are linearly
    independent. This is closely related to what we said above about vectors: two
    linearly independent vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> are enough to build every
    vector in the plane through their linear combinations. The situation with solutions is
    analogous: two "independent enough" solutions are enough to build every solution.
    We won't go further into the theory here; the takeaway is that we need our two solutions
    to be linearly independent, and we need a way to check for that.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: THE WRONSKIAN
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We will check linear independence using <em>the Wronskian</em>. It's exactly the determinant test from above, applied to our
    two solutions.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-wronskian-systems">
    <title>The Wronskian</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{y} = \begin{bmatrix} y_1 \\ y_2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        The <term>Wronskian</term>, <m>W = W(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})</m>, is:
        <me>W = \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \amp y_1 \\ x_2 \amp y_2 \end{bmatrix} \right).</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Notice the two solutions become the <em>columns</em> of the matrix. And finally, we get
    to our main theorem of the section by combining the previous results!
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-wronskian-general-solution">
    <title>The Wronskian and the General Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m> are two solutions to a first-order, linear,
        homogeneous system such that:
        <me>W(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}) \neq 0,</me>
        then the general solution is given by <m>c_1\mathbf{x} + c_2\mathbf{y}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    Let's apply the theorem to our two solutions from above. Recall they were
    <me>\mathbf{y}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      \mathbf{z}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    Placing <m>\mathbf{y}</m> and <m>\mathbf{z}</m> as the columns and computing:
    <md>
      <mrow>W(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{z})
        \amp= \det\left( \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \amp e^{-5t} \\ 2e^{2t} \amp 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix} \right)</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 3e^{2t} \cdot 3e^{-5t} - e^{-5t} \cdot 2e^{2t}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= 9e^{-3t} - 2e^{-3t} = 7e^{-3t}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Notice that <m>W</m> involves <m>t</m> here (that will often happen). The question is
    whether <m>W</m> can ever be <m>0</m>. Since <m>e^{-3t}</m> is <em>never</em> <m>0</m>,
    we have <m>W = 7e^{-3t} \neq 0</m> for any <m>t</m>. So the theorem applies:
    <me>\mathbf{x}=c_1 \begin{bmatrix} 3e^{2t} \\ 2e^{2t} \end{bmatrix}
      + c_2 \begin{bmatrix} e^{-5t} \\ 3e^{-5t} \end{bmatrix}</me>
    is the general solution (it captures all of the solutions to the system).
  </p>

  <p>
    On the next page, we'll run this whole process from start to finish on a new system:
    verify two solutions, check the Wronskian, and write down the general solution.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

